import{_ as t,o as r,c as o,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BxGwJL09.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Workers and Telemetry","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"workers.md","filePath":"workers.md","lastUpdated":null}'),a={name:"workers.md"};function n(l,e,i,h,d,c){return r(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="Workers-and-Telemetry" tabindex="-1">Workers and Telemetry <a class="header-anchor" href="#Workers-and-Telemetry" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Workers and Telemetry {#Workers-and-Telemetry}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Each notebook runs in a worker: a Julia process of its own on this machine, plus one on each region its cells use. The <strong>worker pill</strong> in the top bar shows the notebook&#39;s workers and their state. Click it to open the <strong>worker dock</strong>, a drawer along the bottom of the page.</p><p><img class="slate-shot" src="https://github.com/kahliburke/KaimonSlate.jl/releases/download/docs-assets/worker-dock.png" alt=""></p><h2 id="The-worker-dock" tabindex="-1">The worker dock <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-worker-dock" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The worker dock {#The-worker-dock}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The dock has three columns: every worker of the notebook, the selected worker&#39;s controls and details, and its <strong>Telemetry</strong> or <strong>Log</strong>. Drag the dock&#39;s top edge to resize it; <strong>✕</strong> closes it.</p><p>The controls depend on the worker:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Control</th><th style="text-align:right;">Shown for</th><th style="text-align:right;">What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">⟲ Restart</td><td style="text-align:right;">every worker</td><td style="text-align:right;">Restarts the process. A region keeps its allocation.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">■ Shut down</td><td style="text-align:right;">a running worker</td><td style="text-align:right;">Stops it. On a remote host the process is removed.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">⏏ Release node</td><td style="text-align:right;">a scheduler region holding a node</td><td style="text-align:right;">Gives the node back to the scheduler.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">⏏ Cancel request</td><td style="text-align:right;">a scheduler region waiting in the queue</td><td style="text-align:right;">Withdraws the request.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">▶ Start</td><td style="text-align:right;">a region with no worker</td><td style="text-align:right;">Starts one now rather than at the next run.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">run location</td><td style="text-align:right;">the notebook&#39;s main worker</td><td style="text-align:right;">Where the notebook runs; <strong>change ▾</strong> picks another.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Under the controls are the worker&#39;s details: host and how it is reached, its ports and process, whether it was started fresh or adopted warm, its data and environment directories, and, on a scheduler region, the walltime left, how long it has been idle and when it will be released, and how far its clock is from the hub&#39;s.</p><p><strong>Recent messages</strong> lists the worker&#39;s six newest log records. Click one to open the Log tab at that line.</p><p>While a worker starts, the pill shows the current step, and the dock shows the package precompile&#39;s progress and its build log.</p><h2 id="log" tabindex="-1">Log <a class="header-anchor" href="#log" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Log&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <strong>Log</strong> tab reads the worker&#39;s own log file, page by page, however large it is. The level chips filter by severity, and the search covers the whole file, not only what is on screen. <strong>☰ → 🪵 Worker log</strong> opens the dock at this tab. Before a region&#39;s worker has written a log, the tab shows the trace of acquiring its node.</p><h2 id="telemetry" tabindex="-1">Telemetry <a class="header-anchor" href="#telemetry" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Telemetry&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <strong>Telemetry</strong> tab charts the worker&#39;s resource use. <strong>1m</strong>, <strong>5m</strong>, <strong>15m</strong> and <strong>All</strong> set the range.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Tiles</strong> for CPU, memory, each GPU, garbage collection, compilation, threads, open files and free disk.</p></li><li><p><strong>Timeline</strong>: every cell run as a bar, with a lane for garbage collections. Click a run to go to its cell; a profiled run opens its profile. <strong>Minor GC</strong> and <strong>Full GC</strong> run a collection now.</p></li><li><p><strong>CPU and memory</strong> over time. On a scheduler node the CPU chart is scaled to the job&#39;s allowance, and memory is drawn against the job&#39;s limit.</p></li><li><p><strong>GPU</strong> utilisation and memory, all GPUs together or each on its own.</p></li><li><p><strong>I/O, pressure and the Julia runtime</strong>: disk and network rates, how long work waited for CPU, memory or I/O, and the heap, allocation rate and memo store.</p></li></ul><p><img class="slate-shot" src="https://github.com/kahliburke/KaimonSlate.jl/releases/download/docs-assets/worker-timeline.png" alt=""></p><p>GPU figures come from NVML, so they need an NVIDIA GPU. The host and job figures (per-core use, I/O, pressure, the job&#39;s limits) are read from Linux and are left out on other systems.</p><p>The telemetry of each notebook is also written to disk, one file per day under the cache home&#39;s <code>telemetry</code> folder, and kept for 14 days. <code>telemetry_log</code> (on by default) and <code>telemetry_keep_days</code> in <code>slate.json</code> change that; see <a href="./hub">Running the Hub</a>.</p><h2 id="Across-notebooks" tabindex="-1">Across notebooks <a class="header-anchor" href="#Across-notebooks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Across notebooks {#Across-notebooks}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The hub&#39;s front page has a <strong>Worker activity</strong> panel listing every worker the hub runs, for every notebook. Clicking a row opens the same telemetry view.</p><h2 id="Dedicated-threads" tabindex="-1">Dedicated threads <a class="header-anchor" href="#Dedicated-threads" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dedicated threads {#Dedicated-threads}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A worker keeps two small pools of threads of its own for its machinery: one answers the hub (status, cancel, telemetry, transfers) and one serves the page (live re-renders, table pages, completions). A cell that keeps every <code>:default</code> thread busy then cannot stall either. They come from the <a href="https://github.com/kahliburke/DedicatedThreads.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">DedicatedThreads</a> package. <strong>Dedicated worker threads</strong> in a notebook&#39;s <a href="./settings">settings</a> turns them off for that notebook, and <code>dedicated_threads = false</code> in <code>slate.json</code> for every notebook. The worker then runs its machinery on Julia&#39;s <code>:interactive</code> and <code>:default</code> pools.</p>',22)])])}const k=t(a,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,k as default};
